@@s042 Example (verified)
@@s043 element by element
@@s044 inner product / matrix product
@@s045 power, element by element
@@s046 division with the decimal point
@@s047 integer division (truncated)
@@s048 Python lists ≠ NumPy arrays
@@s049 On <b>lists</b> the same symbols do something else: <code>[1, 2, 3] * 2</code> → <code>[1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3]</code> (repeats) and <code>[1, 2] + [3, 4]</code> → <code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code> (joins). With <code>np.array</code>: <code>[2, 4, 6]</code> and <code>[4, 6]</code>. To do calculations, first <code>np.array(...)</code>.
@@s050 * @ / // on an array, and * on a list
@@s051 Building the design matrix
@@s052 The design matrix of a line has a column of 1s next to the column of the xs. NumPy has several functions to "put together" arrays, and not all of them put the vectors as columns.
@@s053 The column of 1s next to the column of the xs
@@s054 Both put the vectors <b>as columns</b> one next to the other: the 3 × 2 design matrix of a line comes out, rows [1, xᵢ] (see <a href="w04-polinomi.html">polynomials</a>).
@@s055 With 1D vectors <code>hstack</code> <b>joins them in a row</b>: a single vector of 6 numbers. No error, but it is not a matrix.
@@s056 np.c_ versus np.hstack
@@s057 Other silent traps
@@s058 The worst traps are those without an error message: the code runs, the result is wrong. Two classics: assigning an array to another name does not copy it, and an array of integers truncates the decimals.
@@s059 <code>c = b</code> does not copy: it gives a second name <b>to the same array</b>. For an independent copy: <code>c = b.copy()</code>.
@@s060 The array is of integers (<code>dtype int64</code>), so 2.7 is <b>truncated</b> to 2. If decimals will go in, create it with decimals: <code>np.array([1., 2., 3.])</code> or <code>dtype=float</code>.
@@s061 A 3 × 3 covariance for 2 features makes no sense: <code>np.cov</code> by default treats the <b>rows</b> as variables. With the data by rows: <code>np.cov(X, rowvar=False)</code> → [[4, 9], [9, 21]]. That one divides by <b>N − 1</b>; the course notes (DW) divide by <b>N</b>: <code>np.cov(X, rowvar=False, bias=True)</code> → [[2.67, 6], [6, 14]], equal to <code>Xc.T @ Xc / N</code> with the centred data.
@@s062 Two names for the same array
@@s063 Errors with a message
@@s064 These at least stop with a message. The message already says what is wrong: "Singular matrix" means determinant zero, "cannot reshape" means that the number of cells does not match.
@@s065 The second row is twice the first: determinant 0, the inverse does not exist (<a href="w03-sistemi-lineari.html">linear systems</a>). In least squares it happens when two columns of the design matrix are dependent. <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code> still gives a solution.
@@s066 6 numbers do not fill 4 × 2 = 8 cells. The product of the new dimensions must equal the number of elements: <code>reshape(2, 3)</code> or <code>reshape(3, 2)</code>.
@@s067 6 numbers do not fill 4 × 2
